e processed. You cannot visit a POST route directly by typing it into your browser's address bar—it has to be triggered by an HTML form or a frontend network request.Let's build a working POST route together.Step 1: Install the required parser middlewareBy default, Express cannot read form text or JSON out of the box. We need to add an app.use() statement to allow Express to read incoming data.Update your code in VS Code to look exactly like this:javascriptconst express = require("express");
const app = express();
const port = 8080;

// Middleware to parse form data (Mandatory for app.post)
app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: true }));

// 1. GET Route: Shows the submission form
app.get("/register", (req, res) => {
    res.send(`
        <form action="/register" method="POST">
            <input type="text" name="username" placeholder="Enter username" required />
            <button type="submit">Submit Data</button>
        </form>
    `);
});

// 2. POST Route: Receives the submitted data
app.post("/register", (req, res) => {
    const newUsername = req.body.username; // Reads the input field value
    res.send(`Successfully registered user: ${newUsername}`);
});

app.listen(port, () => {
    console.log(`Server is listening on port ${port}`);
});
